1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,094
Pull one brick out of a Lego
tower and push a new one in.

2
00:00:04,094 --> 00:00:06,193
Swap one runner in a relay team.

3
00:00:06,193 --> 00:00:08,145
Change one bead on a bracelet.

4
00:00:08,545 --> 00:00:12,837
In each one, a single piece is swapped,
and the whole thing stays standing.

5
00:00:13,287 --> 00:00:15,560
Diya sees it in the college corridor.

6
00:00:15,560 --> 00:00:18,445
A relay team hands the
baton to a fresh runner.

7
00:00:18,795 --> 00:00:21,048
One runner out, one runner in.

8
00:00:21,048 --> 00:00:22,617
The team never stops.

9
00:00:23,117 --> 00:00:24,833
Benzene does exactly this.

10
00:00:24,833 --> 00:00:29,348
By the end, you will see how it swaps
a hydrogen for a new group, step

11
00:00:29,348 --> 00:00:30,248
by step.

12
00:00:30,737 --> 00:00:32,144
So what is the swap?

13
00:00:32,494 --> 00:00:35,161
Benzene's ring holds six pi
electrons above and below it.

14
00:00:35,161 --> 00:00:36,776
That cloud is rich in electrons.

15
00:00:37,126 --> 00:00:38,674
So it attracts electrophiles.

16
00:00:38,674 --> 00:00:40,510
An electrophile is electron loving.

17
00:00:40,510 --> 00:00:42,640
It is positive, or short of electrons.

18
00:00:42,990 --> 00:00:46,223
The electrophile takes
the place of one hydrogen.

19
00:00:46,223 --> 00:00:48,918
This is called electrophilic substitution.

20
00:00:49,318 --> 00:00:51,339
Why substitution and not addition?

21
00:00:51,339 --> 00:00:54,743
Because swapping keeps the
special ring of shared electrons.

22
00:00:55,143 --> 00:01:00,070
Addition would break that ring, and the
molecule would lose its extra stability.

23
00:01:00,720 --> 00:01:04,574
Let's watch the swap on our
molecule bench, with chlorine.

24
00:01:04,924 --> 00:01:05,824
Step one.

25
00:01:05,759 --> 00:01:07,317
Make the electrophile.

26
00:01:07,317 --> 00:01:11,015
Anhydrous aluminium chloride
pulls on a chlorine molecule.

27
00:01:11,415 --> 00:01:13,515
The chlorine molecule splits.

28
00:01:13,515 --> 00:01:18,169
We get a chlorine cation, C l
plus, and A l C l four minus.

29
00:01:18,569 --> 00:01:19,469
Step two.

30
00:01:19,276 --> 00:01:22,931
Two pi electrons from the ring
reach out and grab the C l plus.

31
00:01:22,931 --> 00:01:23,991
Watch the arrow.

32
00:01:24,391 --> 00:01:27,398
Now one ring carbon holds both H and C l.

33
00:01:27,398 --> 00:01:29,057
It becomes s p three.

34
00:01:29,057 --> 00:01:33,318
This positive ion is called the
sigma complex, or arenium ion.

35
00:01:33,718 --> 00:01:36,706
The ring of shared electrons
is broken for a moment.

36
00:01:36,706 --> 00:01:39,815
The plus charge spreads over
three carbons by resonance.

37
00:01:40,215 --> 00:01:41,138
Step three.

38
00:01:41,138 --> 00:01:44,880
A l C l four minus pulls off
the hydrogen as a proton.

39
00:01:45,280 --> 00:01:47,508
The two electrons fall back into the ring.

40
00:01:47,508 --> 00:01:48,886
The ring is whole again.

41
00:01:48,886 --> 00:01:51,547
Chlorobenzene is made, and
the catalyst comes back.

42
00:01:52,197 --> 00:01:53,704
Compare the two paths.

43
00:01:54,104 --> 00:01:57,062
Substitution keeps the
ring of six pi electrons.

44
00:01:57,062 --> 00:01:59,292
The product is aromatic and stable.

45
00:01:59,692 --> 00:02:02,814
Addition would leave a ring
with only four pi electrons.

46
00:02:02,814 --> 00:02:04,875
Not aromatic, so much less stable.

47
00:02:05,275 --> 00:02:06,423
Pause and think.

48
00:02:06,423 --> 00:02:09,539
In the sigma complex, is
the ring still aromatic?

49
00:02:12,739 --> 00:02:13,639
No!

50
00:02:13,163 --> 00:02:17,541
One carbon is s p three, so the
electrons cannot go all the way round.

51
00:02:17,541 --> 00:02:19,964
That is why it quickly loses a proton.

52
00:02:20,614 --> 00:02:23,849
Now the five big swaps,
one link at a time.

53
00:02:24,199 --> 00:02:25,099
Link one.

54
00:02:25,072 --> 00:02:25,972
Nitration.

55
00:02:25,934 --> 00:02:30,617
Concentrated nitric acid and concentrated
sulphuric acid make the nitronium

56
00:02:30,617 --> 00:02:32,267
ion, NO two plus.

57
00:02:32,267 --> 00:02:33,672
Nitrobenzene forms.

58
00:02:34,422 --> 00:02:35,322
Link two.

59
00:02:35,168 --> 00:02:36,068
Halogenation.

60
00:02:36,041 --> 00:02:40,546
Chlorine with anhydrous ferric chloride
or aluminium chloride gives chlorobenzene.

61
00:02:41,296 --> 00:02:42,196
Link three.

62
00:02:41,991 --> 00:02:42,891
Sulphonation.

63
00:02:42,717 --> 00:02:45,878
Fuming sulphuric acid, called
oleum, gives benzenesulphonic acid.

64
00:02:45,878 --> 00:02:47,473
The electrophile is SO three.

65
00:02:48,223 --> 00:02:49,123
Link four.

66
00:02:49,042 --> 00:02:50,670
Friedel Crafts alkylation.

67
00:02:50,670 --> 00:02:54,762
Methyl chloride with anhydrous
aluminium chloride adds a methyl group.

68
00:02:54,762 --> 00:02:55,769
Toluene forms.

69
00:02:56,519 --> 00:02:57,419
Link five.

70
00:02:57,318 --> 00:02:58,862
Friedel Crafts acylation.

71
00:02:58,862 --> 00:03:02,923
Acetyl chloride with anhydrous
aluminium chloride adds CH three CO.

72
00:03:02,923 --> 00:03:04,135
Acetophenone forms.

73
00:03:04,885 --> 00:03:05,949
Remember this.

74
00:03:05,949 --> 00:03:10,024
Make the electrophile, form the
sigma complex, lose a proton.

75
00:03:10,724 --> 00:03:12,540
Now, one worked example.

76
00:03:12,540 --> 00:03:15,036
Nitration of benzene, step by step.

77
00:03:15,336 --> 00:03:16,236
Step one.

78
00:03:16,097 --> 00:03:18,861
Sulphuric acid gives a
proton to nitric acid.

79
00:03:18,861 --> 00:03:22,776
Water leaves, and the nitronium
ion, NO two plus, is born.

80
00:03:23,526 --> 00:03:24,426
Step two.

81
00:03:24,158 --> 00:03:26,750
The pi electrons of the
ring attack NO two plus.

82
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:28,515
Step three.

83
00:03:28,515 --> 00:03:30,379
The sigma complex forms.

84
00:03:30,379 --> 00:03:34,504
One carbon is s p three and
holds both H and NO two.

85
00:03:35,254 --> 00:03:36,154
Step four.

86
00:03:36,076 --> 00:03:38,984
The hydrogen sulphate
ion pulls off the proton.

87
00:03:39,734 --> 00:03:41,441
The ring is restored.

88
00:03:41,441 --> 00:03:45,499
Nitrobenzene, C six H five
NO two, is the product.

89
00:03:46,149 --> 00:03:48,014
Can benzene ever add things?

90
00:03:48,014 --> 00:03:50,175
Yes, but only when pushed hard.

91
00:03:50,575 --> 00:03:54,950
With hydrogen and a nickel catalyst,
at high temperature and pressure,

92
00:03:54,950 --> 00:03:56,518
it becomes cyclohexane.

93
00:03:56,918 --> 00:03:59,767
In ultraviolet light, three
chlorine molecules add.

94
00:03:59,767 --> 00:04:02,737
We get benzene hexachloride,
C six H six C l six.

95
00:04:03,137 --> 00:04:07,576
And benzene burns in air with a sooty
flame, giving carbon dioxide and

96
00:04:07,576 --> 00:04:08,476
water.

97
00:04:08,837 --> 00:04:10,298
Now, the exam trap.

98
00:04:10,648 --> 00:04:15,164
Students say the electrophile in
nitration is nitric acid itself, or the

99
00:04:15,164 --> 00:04:16,144
nitrate ion.

100
00:04:16,544 --> 00:04:17,444
Wrong.

101
00:04:17,126 --> 00:04:21,543
Sulphuric acid turns nitric acid
into the nitronium ion, NO two plus.

102
00:04:21,543 --> 00:04:24,043
That positive ion is the real attacker.

103
00:04:24,693 --> 00:04:26,013
Where is this asked?

104
00:04:26,013 --> 00:04:30,557
Questions on the electrophile in each
reaction, and on the three step mechanism.

105
00:04:31,157 --> 00:04:32,224
Exam level now.

106
00:04:32,224 --> 00:04:36,121
Benzene is treated with chlorine and
anhydrous aluminium chloride in the

107
00:04:36,121 --> 00:04:37,021
dark.

108
00:04:36,627 --> 00:04:39,873
In a second flask, with
chlorine in ultraviolet light.

109
00:04:39,873 --> 00:04:41,826
What are the two main products?

110
00:04:41,826 --> 00:04:42,938
Pause and solve.

111
00:04:48,338 --> 00:04:49,238
The answer is A.

112
00:04:49,187 --> 00:04:50,924
Chlorobenzene, then benzene hexachloride.

113
00:04:51,424 --> 00:04:54,440
With aluminium chloride, C l plus forms.

114
00:04:54,440 --> 00:04:56,172
It swaps one hydrogen.

115
00:04:56,172 --> 00:04:57,784
That is substitution.

116
00:04:58,284 --> 00:05:02,032
In ultraviolet light, three chlorine
molecules add across the ring.

117
00:05:02,032 --> 00:05:03,147
That is addition.

118
00:05:03,647 --> 00:05:05,056
B swaps the order.

119
00:05:05,056 --> 00:05:08,500
C and D forget that the
conditions decide the path.

120
00:05:09,100 --> 00:05:10,640
Here is your recap.

121
00:05:10,640 --> 00:05:12,223
Screenshot this card.

122
00:05:15,423 --> 00:05:17,609
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

123
00:05:18,009 --> 00:05:18,909
One.

124
00:05:18,458 --> 00:05:21,522
What are the three steps of
electrophilic substitution?

125
00:05:22,322 --> 00:05:23,222
Two.

126
00:05:22,668 --> 00:05:24,473
What is the electrophile in sulphonation?

127
00:05:25,273 --> 00:05:26,173
Three.

128
00:05:25,830 --> 00:05:28,682
Why does benzene prefer
substitution to addition?

129
00:05:29,582 --> 00:05:33,418
Next time: why does a second
group choose its seat on the ring?

130
00:05:33,418 --> 00:05:35,534
We find out in directive influence.
